---
/**
 * The shell every page of this site is served under: the wordmark, where the
 * reader currently is, the site's own navigation, search, and the theme switch.
 *
 * It replaces a lone underlined link sitting in the site-navigation slot, which
 * read as a stray link rather than as a header and said different things on
 * different pages. This is a documentation shell and not a marketing bar, so
 * there is still no call to action — but search is not one, and neither is the
 * navigation below it. The rule both used to be excluded by ("three jobs and no
 * fourth") was aimed at things a reader did not come for. A docs reader who
 * arrived to read one document is exactly the reader who then needs to find the
 * next one, and the only element on the page that is present while they are
 * reading is this strip.
 *
 * ---- the site navigation ----------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Three destinations, the same three on every page: Reference, Providers,
 * Archetypes. They were links at the foot of `/` and nowhere else, which is the
 * one place a reader who has finished reading a README will not look. The
 * header is the element that is present on every page, so it is where they go.
 *
 * The workbench is not among them and will not be: #534 records that it is not
 * served as a public surface, and the maintainer ruled the same on a Storybook
 * link. This list is the site's own pages.
 *
 * `aria-current="page"` marks the section the reader is inside, on a link among
 * sibling links — which is exactly the case the note on the breadcrumb below
 * says the attribute does work in, and the case the rail already uses it for.
 * A reference document therefore carries two of them: this nav's `Reference`,
 * and the rail's entry for the package being read. They are in differently
 * labelled landmarks and they are both true — the reader is in Reference, and
 * in that package — so the ambiguity the breadcrumb note avoids (two markers
 * for the same trail, one of them not even a link) does not arise here.
 *
 * The section is derived from `Astro.url.pathname` rather than passed in, so a
 * page added later is marked without being told to mark itself, and a page
 * cannot claim a section it is not served from. The `BASE_URL` prefix is
 * stripped first: the site ships from the apex, where the prefix is `/` and a
 * comparison against a literal would look identical, and `astro build --base`
 * is what makes the difference observable (#435).
 *
 * Search is on documentation pages only, which is what the `documentation` prop
 * says. It defaults to true — a page added later is documentation unless it
 * says otherwise — and `Base.astro` takes a prop of the same name for the same
 * decision on the indexing side. The two are separate props because the layout
 * cannot pass anything to a component a page renders into its slot, and they
 * are named alike so that a page turning one off and not the other reads as the
 * mistake it is.
 *
 * The whole header is `data-pagefind-ignore`. Its words are the same on every
 * page, so indexed they would be indexed once per document — a query for
 * "reference" or "search" would match the whole site, and the words nearest the
 * top of each page, which is where an excerpt is drawn from, would be this
 * strip rather than the document.
 *
 * Deliberately not sticky. A reference page is a whole README, long enough that
 * a bar following the reader down would be defensible — but it would also have
 * to fight the rail's own sticky offset for the top of the viewport, and the
 * rail is the thing a reader actually navigates a long document with. One
 * sticky element per page.
 *
 * ---- the wordmark and the crumb --------------------------------------------
 *
 * `crumbs` is the path from the site root to this page, not including the root
 * itself, and its last entry is always the page the reader is on. An entry with
 * an `href` is a link; the last one has none, which is what marks it as the
 * page rather than a destination.
 *
 * The wordmark is the root of that path, so it is the trail's first item and
 * sits inside the breadcrumb landmark rather than beside it. A trail whose
 * first named ancestor is `Reference` claims the site has no root.
 *
 * `aria-current="page"` is deliberately absent here. The rail on a reference
 * page marks the current package with it, on a link among sibling links, which
 * is where the attribute does work; a second one on a non-interactive `span` in
 * the header would put two current-page markers in one document and leave a
 * reader to work out which is meant. The leaf is identified structurally
 * instead — last in the trail and not a link — and styled from `:last-child`.
 *
 * On `/` the list is empty and the trail is not rendered at all. There is
 * nowhere for it to return to, the page's own `h1` is already the wordmark at
 * the title rung — `scripts/check-deploy-artifact.mjs` identifies the site's
 * root document by a heading named exactly `Playdeck`, so that `h1` cannot move
 * — and a strip repeating it directly above would be the same word twice in
 * eighty pixels.
 *
 * That check used to carry a second constraint this decision protected: it
 * followed every internal link on `/` in one page context, so the workbench had
 * to be the last one and a header that quietly added a link would have changed
 * what the check meant. It visits each link in a page of its own now (#528), so
 * only the reason above is still doing any work here.
 */
import DocsSearch from './DocsSearch.astro';
import ThemeToggle from './ThemeToggle.astro';

interface Crumb {
  label: string;
  href?: string;
}

interface Props {
  crumbs?: Crumb[];
  documentation?: boolean;
}

const { crumbs = [], documentation = true } = Astro.props;

// Through `import.meta.env.BASE_URL` rather than as a literal, for the reason
// `src/pages/index.astro` gives at length: the prefix is the root today, so a
// hand-written path would be identical and would stop being identical silently
// (#435).
const homeUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const atHome = crumbs.length === 0;

// The section the reader is in: the first segment of this page's path with the
// deployment prefix taken off. `/` and `/design/` are in none of the sections
// listed below, so they simply match nothing.
const path = Astro.url.pathname;
const section = (
  path.startsWith(homeUrl) ? path.slice(homeUrl.length) : path.replace(/^\//, '')
).split('/')[0];

const destinations = [
  { label: 'Reference', segment: 'reference' },
  { label: 'Providers', segment: 'providers' },
  { label: 'Archetypes', segment: 'archetypes' }
];
---

<header class="bar" data-pagefind-ignore>
  <div class="bar__place">
    {
      !atHome && (
        <nav class="bar__crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
          <ol class="bar__trail">
            <li class="bar__crumb">
              <a class="bar__mark" href={homeUrl}>
                Playdeck
              </a>
            </li>
            {crumbs.map(({ label, href }) => (
              <li class="bar__crumb">
                {href === undefined ? <span>{label}</span> : <a href={href}>{label}</a>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </nav>
      )
    }
  </div>

  {
    /* Search and the switch travel together at the far end of the row, and
     * neither shrinks: the trail above is the flexible box. */
  }
  <div class="bar__tools">
    {documentation && <DocsSearch />}
    <ThemeToggle />
  </div>

  {
    /* Last in the strip and last in the source, and it takes a line of its own
     * at every width — see the note on `.bar__nav` below. Reading order and
     * visual order are therefore the same thing at every width, which is what
     * a second row placed with `order` would have cost. */
  }
  <nav class="bar__nav" aria-label="Site">
    <ul class="bar__links">
      {
        destinations.map(({ label, segment }) => (
          <li>
            <a
              href={`${homeUrl}${segment}/`}
              aria-current={segment === section ? 'page' : undefined}
            >
              {label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </nav>
</header>

<style>
  .bar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-5);
    border-block-end: var(--line-width) solid var(--color-line);
  }

  /* The trail is the shrinkable group and the switch is not: at 320px the trail
   * is what gives way, and a switch that had wrapped its label under its track
   * would be the more obvious break. The element is kept even on `/`, where the
   * trail is not rendered, because it is what holds the switch at the far end
   * of a `space-between` row. */
  .bar__place {
    display: flex;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    align-items: baseline;
    /* The trail gives way by being clipped here, not by sliding under the
     * controls beside it. A crumb that cannot shrink does not disappear when
     * the room runs out, and once search joined the strip (#525) `Reference`
     * was overflowing this box and painting under the search button at 360px.
     * Clipping is the give the rule on `.bar__crumb` below already assumes. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .bar__tools {
    display: flex;
    flex: none;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    align-items: center;
  }

  /* The navigation takes a whole line of its own, at every width. Three names
   * cannot share the first line with the trail, search and the switch at 320px
   * — measured, the room left there is under a third of what they need — so
   * the choice is a second line or a disclosure that hides them. The rail is a
   * `<details>` because it is a list per package plus a list per heading,
   * several phone screens of it; three short words are not that. A row that is
   * always visible costs one line of header and needs no summary to open, no
   * state to announce and no script.
   *
   * `flex-basis: 100%` rather than a media query, so the line is the same line
   * at 320px and at 1600px: the reader who resizes finds the navigation where
   * they left it, and the header has one layout to reason about instead of
   * two. It is a flex item of `.bar` rather than a row inside a wrapper so
   * that the trail above keeps the clipping box it already relies on. */
  .bar__nav {
    flex-basis: 100%;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  /* And the names wrap among themselves. They are the site's own words and
   * short, so they fit on one line at 320px with room to spare — but a
   * destination added later, or a reader who has scaled text up, must push the
   * list down rather than push the page sideways. */
  .bar__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-4);
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .bar__links a {
    display: inline-block;
    padding-block: var(--space-1);
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .bar__links a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* Pressed, the fourth state every control on this site carries. */
  .bar__links a:active {
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }

  /* The section the reader is in, marked by the same attribute that tells a
   * screen reader which one it is rather than by a class that only says it
   * visually — as the rail marks the current document. Weight and colour
   * together, because colour alone is not a state. */
  .bar__links a[aria-current='page'] {
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  /* The wordmark is not a heading. `scripts/check-deploy-artifact.mjs`
   * identifies the site's root document in a browser by a heading named exactly
   * `Playdeck`, so a second element answering to that role and name on every
   * page of the site would make the identification ambiguous. It is set in the
   * body rung rather than larger: the loudest thing on a documentation page is
   * the document's own title. That rung has to be asked for, because the trail
   * it now sits inside is set one rung down.
   *
   * Every declaration here is a link's, so the selector carries the element as
   * well as the class: `.bar__crumb a` below would otherwise outrank a bare
   * `.bar__mark` and paint the root of the trail in the ancestors' ink. */
  a.bar__mark {
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    text-decoration: none;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
  }

  a.bar__mark:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  a.bar__mark:active {
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }

  /* The trail truncates rather than wraps or pushes. A package name is one
   * unbroken token — `@playdeck/provider-youtube` is wider than the room a
   * 320px header has left after the wordmark and the switch — and the reader
   * has the whole name in the `h1` immediately underneath, so an ellipsis here
   * costs nothing. */
  .bar__crumbs {
    min-inline-size: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }

  .bar__trail {
    display: flex;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Every crumb but the last keeps its full width, and the last one is the only
   * one allowed to shrink. The ancestors are short words chosen by this site;
   * the leaf is a package name chosen by npm, so it is both the longest and the
   * one the reader can read in full from the `h1` a line below. Letting them all
   * shrink in proportion truncated `Reference` to `Refer…` at 390px to save room
   * a crumb that was already legible did not need — and, measured again when
   * search arrived, to `P…` and `R…` at 360px, which is a trail that has stopped
   * saying anything at all. The clip on `.bar__place` above is what keeps the
   * consequence inside the trail's own box. */
  .bar__crumb {
    flex: none;
    min-inline-size: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The leaf, which is the page the reader is on: last in the trail, and the
   * only entry rendered without an `href`. Weight and ink rather than colour
   * alone, and no `aria-current` — see the note at the top of this file. */
  .bar__crumb:last-child {
    flex: 0 1 auto;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* The separator is generated rather than typed into the markup, so the crumb
   * labels stay the names themselves and a later trail cannot arrive with the
   * slashes baked into its strings. It is NOT hidden from assistive
   * technology: VoiceOver and NVDA both announce generated content, so the
   * trail is heard as the path it draws — root, separator, ancestor, separator,
   * leaf — which is what a breadcrumb is meant to say. Between crumbs only; the
   * root of the path is the first of them and has nothing to be separated
   * from. */
  .bar__crumb + .bar__crumb::before {
    margin-inline-end: var(--space-2);
    content: '/';
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }

  .bar__crumb a {
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .bar__crumb a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* Pressed, the fourth state every control on this site carries. A step down
   * the ink scale rather than a lift: this system has no shadow, and the state
   * has to read the same way from any of the rest colours a link here takes. */
  .bar__crumb a:active {
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }
</style>
